    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "When the shape of the loop and the magnetic properties of the surrounding medium remain fixed, and the magnetic field is proportional to the current, the **magnetic flux density $\\vec{B}$ produced by the current is proportional to the current**."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The magnetic flux $\\Phi$ is also proportional to $B$, so:\n$\\Phi\\propto B\\propto I$."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The relationship can therefore be written as:\n$\\Phi=LI$,\n(12-7)"
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Here, $L$ is the proportionality coefficient relating the current in the loop to the magnetic flux through the loop produced by that current."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "$L$ is called the inductance, or the coefficient of self-induction, of the loop."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "If the shape of the loop remains fixed, the flux changes only because the current changes, and L remains constant during that change, the law of electromagnetic induction and equation (12-7) give:\n$\\mathcal{E}_{is}=-\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}=-L\\frac{\\Delta I}{\\Delta t}$. (12-8)"
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Equation (12-8) shows that **under these conditions, the numerical value of the inductance equals the magnitude of the self-induced electromotive force when the current changes by one unit in one unit of time**."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Like capacitance, inductance depends on the conductor's geometry, including its size and shape."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "When the magnetic properties of the medium do not change with the current, the inductance does not depend directly on the magnitude of the current."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Inductance also depends on the magnetic properties of the medium surrounding the conductor."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "If the permeability $\\mu$ changes with the magnetic field, and the field changes with the current producing it, then the inductance also depends on the current through $\\mu$①."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "① For the relationship between $\\mu$ and the magnetic field, see Chapter Thirteen."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In the International System of Units, the unit of inductance is called the henry."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "If the current changes by 1 ampere in 1 second and produces a self-induced electromotive force of magnitude 1 volt in the conductor, its inductance is 1 henry:\n1 henry $=\\frac{1\\text{volt}}{1\\frac{\\text{ampere}}{\\text{second}}}=1\\frac{\\text{volt}\\cdot\\text{second}}{\\text{ampere}}$."
    }
  ],
